t if removable and if required symbols are still satisfied without this node and its removed descendants. However, the required symbols check might be simplified: if the node's symbol is required, and there is no other retained node with that symbol outside this subtree, then removal would violate. But because we process bottom-up, we can compute for each subtree the symbols present. But we also need to know the global presence of symbols.\n\nSimpler: implement greedy removal with global check. For each node in order, if removable, and all descendants are currently not retained (i.e., we have already decided to remove them), and after removing this node (and its removed descendants) the global set of symbols still covers all required, then remove it (mark as removed). Otherwise, mark as retained. But we need to ensure that when we check descendants, they are not retained. Since we process deeper first, when we consider a node, its children have already been decided. So if any child is retained, then this node cannot be removed because it would remove that child. So condition: all children are removed (i.e., not in retained set). Then we check if removing this node (adding its ID to removed set) would cause any required symbol to become missing. But the required symbols are represented by nodes not in the removed set. So we need to compute per symbol the count of nodes that have that symbol and are not removed. Initially all nodes are retained. We iterate and for each node we consider removal; if we remove, we decrement the count for its symbol. We also need to decrement counts for all descendants? But since we process bottom-up, when we remove a node, its descendants are already removed (because they were deeper and either removed or retained; but if a descendant is retained, we cannot remove this node). So if we remove this node, we only need to decrement the count for its own symbol (since descendants are already removed). But careful: if a descendant was retained