on't matter. So if node's symbol is not required, we can remove it even if count goes to 0. But the condition might also be that we cannot remove a node if it would cause the tree to lose necessary structure? But the problem statement only mentions required_symbols. So I'll assume only required symbols matter.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not required but have children that are retained? Already handled by the child check. So algorithm:\n\n- Parse nodes list: create dict nodes_by_id, children list for each parent, depth from node['depth'].\n- Build initial retained set = all node IDs.\n- Build symbol_counts: for each node, increment count for its symbol.\n- Build a list of node IDs sorted by depth descending, then by ID ascending.\n- For each node_id in that order:\n   node = nodes_by_id[node_id]\n   if not node['removable']: continue (retained remains)\n   # Check if any child retained:\n   has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained_set for child_id in children[node_id])\n   if has_retained_child:\n       continue # must retain this node\n   # Check if removing would cause required symbol shortage:\n   if node['symbol'] in required_set and symbol_counts[node['symbol']] == 1:\n       continue # would lose the only instance of required symbol\n   # Otherwise, remove this node:\n   retained_set.remove(node_id)\n   symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\n   # (children are already removed by previous decisions, so no change for them)\n\nAfter loop, removed_node_ids = [id for id in all_ids if id not in retained_set], we need to output them in the order they were removed? The output contract says \"removed_node_ids\" is an array of strings (presumably in any order? But the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" For retained, they want sorted. For removed, they want the actual removal order (the order in which we removed them). So we need to record removal order. So we can maintain a list removed_order and append node_id when we remove.\n\nSimilarly, retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably lexicographically ascending). The output schema does not specify order but the description implies sorted for retained, removal order for removed.\n\nAlso need to handle the root: it's removable=False, so it will not be removed.\n\nBut there is a nuance: the example payloads have nodes with depth 0,1,2. Required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. The root is always retained. s1 appears in multiple nodes: root's children n1, n4, maybe others. We need to ensure at least one s1 remains. Our algorithm: process depths 2 first (n4,n5,n6). n4: removable=true, parent n2. Children? n4 has no children (depth 2). No retained child. Symbol 's1' is required. At initial, symbol_counts['s1'] is? Let's count: root (root), n1 (s1), n2 (s2), n3 (s0), n4 (s1), n5 (s2), n6 (s0). So s1 count = 2 (n1, n4). So when considering n4, symbol_counts['s1']=2 >1, so we can remove n4. So remove n4. Then symbol_counts['s1'] becomes 1. Next n5: removable=false -> retain. n6: removable=true, symbol s0 (not required), no retained child, so can remove. Remove n6. Then depth 1 nodes: n1, n2, n3. Process order by depth descending, then ID: n1, n2, n3 (since depth=1). For n1: removable=false -> retain. n2: removable=true, children: n4 (already removed), so no retained child. symbol n2's symbol is 's2' (not required), so can remove. Remove n2. n3: removable=false -> retain. Then root: depth0, removable=false. So final removed: n4, n6, n2 (in that removal order). retained: root, n1, n3, n5? Wait n5 is retained (depth2, removable=false). So retained IDs: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']. Now check required symbols: root present, s1 present (n1). Good.\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to ensure that the removal order matches what the fixtures expect. We don't have expected outputs, but the description says \"actual removal order\".\n\nEdge cases: If a node has retained children, we must keep it. That is enforced.\n\nBut what about the possibility that a node is removable but its child is also removable and we might want to remove the child but keep the parent? That is allowed because we process deeper first: we remove child if possible, then later consider parent. If child is removed, then parent has no retained child, so can be removed if conditions hold.\n\nWhat if a node is removable but its symbol is required and there is another node with same symbol that is also removable? Our algorithm might remove one of them, leaving the other. That's fine.\n\nNow, we must also consider that the required_symbols list might have duplicates? The examples show two distinct symbols. We'll treat as set.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Parse case payload: case is the inner object (the payload). It has 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list.\n- Validate: expected fields present. If missing, raise ValueError.\n- Build children adjacency: for each node, add its ID to parent's children list. Ensure root has null parent.\n- For each node, store id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (utility not used).\n- Sort by depth descending, then ID ascending (string comparison). Use sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])).\n- Iterate.\n\nPotential issue: The children of a node might include nodes that are not in the nodes list? But all are present.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure we don't modify input. We'll create a copy of the list? Not necessary, we just read.\n\nReturn a dict with keys 'removed_node_ids' (list in removal order) and 'retained_node_ids' (sorted ascending).\n\nNow, write the solve function. Must be pure, no imports beyond standard library (maybe json for serialization, but function returns dict, json.dumps is not needed). The return should be a JSON-serializable dict.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        children[nid] = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\n    # Initial retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Sort nodes by depth desc, then id asc\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child is retained\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        # Check required symbol\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 1:\n            continue\n        # Remove this node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    # Build result\n    removed_ids = removal_order\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n\nBut wait: In the removal condition, we check if any child is retained. However, children that are not retained may have been removed earlier. That's correct. But what about children that are not yet processed? Since we process deeper first, all children have been processed before the node. So children set in retained reflects final decisions. Good.\n\nOne more nuance: Suppose a node has a child that is removable and we decide to remove that child, but later we might decide to remove the parent. That's okay. But if a child is retained because it is not removable or we didn't remove it, then parent cannot be removed.\n\nNow, check the example with the second fixture: similar structure, should work.\n\nWe also need to consider that the tree may have nodes with multiple children. Our algorithm handles that.\n\nPotential edge: The root might have parent null. We treat that as having no parent, but it's not relevant.\n\nNow, ensure no mutation of input: we are only reading.\n\nWe must also handle invalid input: raise ValueError if missing keys. But the problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should validate that nodes list is present, each node has required fields, required_symbols list present, and that the tree is acyclic? That might be too much. Simplified: check for missing id, parent, symbol, depth, removable fields. If any missing, raise ValueError. Also check that root has parent null and depth 0, but not strictly required? The fixtures show root with depth 0 and parent null. We'll include some validation.\n\nWe'll add basic validation:\n\nrequired_node_keys = {'id','parent','symbol','depth','removable'}\nfor node in nodes:\n    if not required_node_keys.issubset(node.keys()):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid node structure\")\n\nAlso check that required_symbols is a list.\n\nBut the problem likely expects simple validation. We'll implement.\n\nNow, final output: return JSON-like dict.\n\nNow, we need to embed this in a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So whole response must be a JSON object like {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case): ...\"}. The Python source must be a string with proper escaping. So we need to write the function and then put it as a JSON string.\n\nLet's write the implementation string. We'll include proper indentation and newlines. In JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and quotes. We'll write it and then use json.dumps to encode, but we are the respondent, we need to manually provide as a string response. Since we are generating text, we can write the JSON object directly. Ensure the Python source is properly escaped.\n\nI'll write the function definition as a single string with newlines represented as \\n inside the JSON string. But easier: I'll write the entire response as a JSON object literal in the text. I'll use triple quotes? No, it's plain text.\n\nWe'll generate the JSON string:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    removed_ids = removal_order\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_ids,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}\n\nBut we need to ensure the string is valid JSON: escape backslashes and quotes. In the above, we have single quotes inside the string. JSON strings must use double quotes. So we need to escape double quotes inside the string, and also backslashes. However, the Python source contains single quotes for dict keys, so it's fine; we can keep single quotes. But JSON requires double quotes for string delimiters. So the outer JSON string must be double-quoted. Inside, we can have single quotes freely. But if we need to use double quotes inside (e.g., for escaped strings), they need to be escaped. In our code, we used single quotes for keys, so no double quotes. But we have backslashes for newlines: \\n. In JSON, backslash must be escaped, so we need \\\\n. So each \\n becomes \\\\n. Also any existing backslashes would need escaping, but we have none.\n\nSo we will write the implementation string with \\\\n for newlines. We also need to escape any double quotes if present. But we used no double quotes, so fine.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the string does not contain unescaped control characters. \\n is allowed as two-character sequence in JSON string when escaped as \\\\n.\n\nLet's construct the string.\n\nAlso, we should add basic validation as mentioned. Let's include a validation check at start:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    for node in nodes:\n        for key in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable'):\n            if key not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing key: {key}\")\n    # ... rest\n\nBut the raise ValueError with f-string uses double quotes? It's inside single-quoted string? Actually we have double quotes in f-string? In Python, it's fine. In JSON, we need to escape any double quotes. The f-string uses double quotes for the string: f\"Node missing key: {key}\" contains double quotes. So we need to escape those as \\\" in the JSON string. Better to avoid double quotes in the implementation to reduce escaping. We can use single quotes for the f-string: f'Node missing key: {key}' but that uses single quotes. JSON allows single quotes inside a double-quoted string without escaping. However, f-string with single quotes is fine: f'Node missi